import WordMeaning from '../../components/WordMeaning.astro';

const sections = [
	{
		id: 'what-meek-means',
		heading: 'What “meek” means',
		bodyHtml: `<p><em>Meek</em> means <strong>gentle and humble because you trust our Father, not because you are weak</strong>. A meek person does not grab, force or retaliate; they have strength and choose to keep it under His control. In modern English &ldquo;meek&rdquo; can sound timid. In the Bible it is the opposite of timid: it is the settled confidence of someone who does not need to fight for their place, because their Father has already given it.</p><p>Jesus made it a promise: &ldquo;Blessed are the meek, for they will inherit the earth&rdquo; (Matthew 5:5). The meek do not seize the earth. They are given it.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'hebrew-and-greek',
		heading: 'The Hebrew and Greek words behind “meek”',
		bodyHtml: `<p>The Greek word in Matthew 5:5 is <em>praus</em> (the noun is <em>praut&#275;s</em>, &ldquo;meekness&rdquo;). Outside the Bible it was used of a strong horse that had been trained to the bridle: all of its power, none of its wildness. Paul uses the noun of Jesus Himself: &ldquo;I, Paul, myself appeal to you by the meekness and gentleness of Christ&rdquo; (2 Corinthians 10:1).</p><p>Behind the Beatitude stands the Hebrew <em>anav</em>, the humble or lowly person who depends on our Father. Jesus is quoting Psalm 37:11, which Father&rsquo;s Heart Bible&trade; renders, &ldquo;But the humble will inherit the land and delight in abundant peace,&rdquo; and the FHB footnote on that verse makes the link: Jesus draws this promise into his Beatitudes.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'meek-not-weak',
		heading: 'Meek is not weak: Moses and Jesus',
		bodyHtml: `<p>The two people Scripture most clearly calls meek are its strongest leaders. Of Moses, who stood before Pharaoh and led a nation through the sea, it says: &ldquo;Now the man Moses was very humble, more than anyone else on the face of the earth&rdquo; (Numbers 12:3). The Hebrew word there is the same <em>anav</em>.</p><p>And Jesus describes Himself with the Greek word: &ldquo;Put my yoke on you and learn from me, since I&rsquo;m gentle and humble in heart, and you&rsquo;ll find rest for your souls&rdquo; (Matthew 11:29). The One who calmed storms and drove out demons called Himself gentle. Meekness is power that serves rather than dominates.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'why-the-meek-inherit',
		heading: 'Why the meek inherit the earth',
		bodyHtml: `<p>An inheritance is given to children by a father. The meek inherit because they are the Father&rsquo;s children who have stopped fighting for what He has promised to give. Isaiah pictures the coming King defending them: &ldquo;with righteousness he will judge the poor, and decide with equity for the meek of the earth&rdquo; (Isaiah 11:4).</p><p>That is why meekness can rest instead of strive. &ldquo;Seek our Father, all you humble of the land,&rdquo; says Zephaniah (2:3). The meek are not the forgotten; they are the ones their Father has promised to remember.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'growing-in-meekness',
		heading: 'How to grow in meekness',
		bodyHtml: `<p>Meekness is not a personality type; it is fruit our Father&rsquo;s Spirit grows: &ldquo;love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control&rdquo; (Galatians 5:22&ndash;23). It shows up in how we treat people: &ldquo;with all humility and gentleness, with patience, bearing with one another in love&rdquo; (Ephesians 4:2).</p><p>Peter describes it as a beauty no one can take away: &ldquo;the undying beauty of a gentle, quiet spirit, precious in our Father&rsquo;s sight&rdquo; (1 Peter 3:4). It grows as we learn from Jesus, the one who is gentle and humble in heart.</p>`,
	},
];

